## What needs explaining

1. **Why this model was tried.** The raw tone averages are lower for older signings in both periods. The exploratory question is whether the post-signing gap persists when the model accounts for differences in prior Guardian attention, recorded market value and season, and after the score adjustment for article type and removal of sentences containing double quotation marks. Each signing contributes one mean sentence score per period. The quote filter and the requirement of five sentences in each period leave 793 signings, including 65 aged 30+. The baseline rows use the 820-signing sample. [Saved specifications](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/a1_h2b_controls.csv) · [Model and preprocessing](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/a1_h2b_controls.py#L24)
2. **How to read the coefficient.** Start with a hypothetical pair from one season, both valued at EUR 10 million and both named in 20 Guardian articles before signing. Their season baseline, log market value and log(1 + prior count) are identical. In the fitted model the age indicator changes from 0 to 1, so their predicted after-signing scores differ by −0.0349. Two separate regressions estimate tone levels before and after. The direct change regression answers whether the two age gaps differ. Explain how the model learns a common age coefficient across all observed signings at once and why this model-based comparison depends on overlap in the observed inputs. [Model code](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/a1_h2b_controls.py#L53)
3. **What the evidence adds.** On the same 820 signings, adding logged market value to season and prior coverage changes the post-signing estimate from −0.0279 to −0.0344 and the permutation p-value from 0.1048 to 0.0482. The lower post-signing estimate persists and the nominal evidence depends on the model. Holding market value fixed narrows the question because age may also shape value. Explain why the p-value is exploratory. The shuffle holds the outcomes and covariates in place and reallocates the age labels within season, preserving seasonal age counts while allowing age to relate differently to the covariates. The 793-signing all-controls estimate is −0.0349 after (p = 0.0390), −0.0153 before (p = 0.3511) and −0.0196 for the direct difference in changes (p = 0.3455). The direction of the post-signing estimate supplies the exploratory observation. Whether signing widened the age gap and why the gap exists remain open. [Saved results](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/a1_h2b_controls.csv) · [Issue 05 evidence](../evidence_and_sources.md#exploratory-h2b-before--and-after-signing-tone-levels)
4. **What could benefit from a further check.** Discuss whether a view of the joint distribution of prior coverage and recorded market value by age, within relevant seasons, would help assess where the model is making comparisons close to observed data. The [per-signing export](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/a1_h2b_signings.csv) comes from the 820-signing baseline and could support a baseline diagnostic. Exact overlap for the 793 signings requires their membership, which the export does not provide. The full article bodies, sentence inputs and score caches remain unavailable at their documented paths. Discuss any additional analysis or check with the user before running it. Dispatch an approved script rerun to a fresh-context agent with `fork_turns="none"`.

## Figure to develop

First explain the story the reader should get from the plot in one sentence. Proposed message: the estimated post-signing gap stays negative across the main RoBERTa specifications, and including market value changes the strength of the statistical evidence.

- **Candidate.** Make a compact horizontal dot plot from the after-period RoBERTa rows in [a1_h2b_controls.csv](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/a1_h2b_controls.csv). Use four clearly labelled rows: raw age difference, season and prior coverage, season and prior coverage plus market value, and the full adjustment with quotes removed. Place a vertical reference at zero and label the axis “Tone score difference, 30+ minus under 30”. Mark the sample sizes alongside each row. The second-to-third comparison isolates adding market value on the same signings. The final row also changes sentence processing and the sample, so show it separately from the sequence of covariate additions. Keep nominal p-values secondary to the estimates. If using the existing bars, label them as each estimate ± 1.96 standard deviations of the within-season shuffle distribution. The caption should explain that the raw row uses a simple mean difference and season-restricted shuffling.
- **Why existing plots are insufficient for this job.** The [current dot-figure implementation](../../notebooks/plot_guardian_text_findings.py#L173) displays only the raw and all-controls specifications for each instrument. That comparison combines several changes and skips the same-sample market-value step. The [historical dot-figure footer](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/a1_h2b_figure.py#L105) assigns season adjustment to all models. The raw H2b estimate is a simple difference between means, with season restriction in its age-label shuffle. The [adjusted time arc](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/a1_h2b_arc.py) displays smoothed monthly averages. Its printed age gaps come from period-level comparisons, so the vertical distance between the lines at any one month should be read as a monthly descriptive difference.
- **For explaining the model to the user.** Consider a separate labelled illustration of the hypothetical matched inputs and the age coefficient if the numerical walkthrough needs visual support. Any plot that uses actual fitted slopes or shows observed overlap requires a discussion of the relevant inputs and the evidence available. An explanatory illustration can be useful during review even if the paper figure uses the saved estimates.

